<main class="shell container-xxl px-3 px-lg-4">
    <section id="overview" class="page-intro">
        <div class="section-heading row g-3 align-items-end">
            <div class="col-lg-7">
                <div class="eyebrow">Program Snapshot</div>
                <h1>Start with the few facts that explain why Landsat matters</h1>
            </div>
            <div class="col-lg-5">
                <p class="body-copy">
                    Landsat is the NASA-USGS program that has observed Earth's land surface continuously across more than
                    five decades, creating a consistent record for agriculture, water, forests, cities, hazards, climate
                    resilience, and environmental policy.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row g-4 metrics-grid">
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="metric fade-up h-100">
                    <strong>50+ years</strong>
                    <span>Continuous land observations from 1972 to 2026.</span>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="metric fade-up h-100">
                    <strong>8</strong>
                    <span>Successful Landsat satellites have reached orbit.</span>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="metric fade-up h-100">
                    <strong>$25.6B</strong>
                    <span>Estimated economic value of Landsat imagery in 2023, according to USGS.</span>
                </article>
            </div>
            <div class="col-sm-6 col-xl-3">
                <article class="metric fade-up h-100">
                    <strong>&lt; 1.4 days</strong>
                    <span>Global median revisit frequency for HLS Version 2.0 in 2025 with five satellites.</span>
                </article>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="lesson-band mt-4">
            <article class="lesson-card">
                <div class="eyebrow">Learning Objectives</div>
                <h3>By the end of this page</h3>
                <ul class="learning-list">
                    <li>Explain why Landsat's long, calibrated record is different from a single satellite image.</li>
                    <li>Describe why 30 m land observations are useful even though they are not street-level imagery.</li>
                    <li>Name two decisions that benefit from Landsat's optical and thermal measurements.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
            <article class="lesson-card">
                <div class="eyebrow">Tutor Check</div>
                <h3>Why is continuity valuable?</h3>
                <p class="body-copy">A city planner can compare a 1980s scene with a modern Landsat 8 or 9 scene because the archive is systematic, calibrated, and globally organized. The power is the comparable record, not one dramatic image.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
        <div class="source-note mt-4">
            <strong>Source trail:</strong> NASA and USGS jointly manage Landsat. NASA's Landsat multimedia pages describe public-domain imagery and animations, while USGS product pages document current Collection 2 science products and no-cost data access.
        </div>
        <div class="page-nav">
            <a class="button" href="/briefing">All chapters</a>
            <a class="button button-primary" href="/briefing/foundations">Next: foundations</a>
        </div>
    </section>
</main>
